  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWater vapor loop shows split flow with the main upper low over\nID/UT, and west to southwest flow over the Plains. Weak frontal\nboundary sinking down over our region has brought a few weak\nrain showers. Even with cold air advection behind the front, \nplenty of clouds into tonight should help keep temps in the\nupper 40s into the 50s. The upper low will come eastward into SD\nand Neb tomorrow, bringing clouds but much of the precipitation\nremains south of our CWA. The weakening upper low pulls further\nsoutheast of us on Monday, with a blocking ridge over the Plains\nmid-week. Most of the ensemble members bring a shortwave over\nthe top of the ridge late Wednesday and into Thursday, with\nridging to weak southwesterly flow setting up after that.\n\n...Light precipitation chances this evening...\n\nLight rain showers near the frontal boundary coming down have\ndissipated, but many of the CAMs, including the HRRR and RAP,\nhave redevelopment between 00Z and 03Z a bit further south.\nAmounts and impacts will be minimal, a few hundredths at most,\nbut can't rule out a few sprinkles before midnight. The main\nupper low still out over the Rockies starts to come east into\nthe Plains, but ensemble members are in good agreement on\nimpacts remaining to the south of our area. Only locations right\nnear the SD border have any probability of 0.01 of rain, and \nless than 20 percent at that. Sunday and the first part of the\nwork week look to remain dry, but a shortwave coming out late\nWednesday into Thursday could bring some light precipitation.\nDecent probabilities of around 60 percent for at least 0.01 of\nrain, but very low chances for anything more than half an inch.",
